<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tilokarat</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1409">1409</a>–<a href="/years/1487">1487</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Tilokaraj</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tilokarat was king of Lan Na, in what is now northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, from 1441 to 1487, and its most powerful ruler. He waged long wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>, extended Lan Na's power over Nan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrae">Phrae</a>, and was a great patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a>. In 1477 he sponsored a council of monks at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-chet-yot">Wat Chet Yot</a> in Chiang Mai to revise the Pali scriptures, counted in Thai tradition as the Eighth Buddhist Council.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tilokarat.jpg" alt="Tilokarat" width="320" height="497" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Security Thainam · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tilokaraj.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>According to the Chiang Mai Chronicle, Tilokarat was born as Thao Lok, a name meaning sixth, the sixth child of King Samfangkaen of Lan Na. His father first gave him Mueang Phrao Wang Hin, the present-day Phrao district of Chiang Mai province, to rule, but after he disobeyed his father's command he was banished to Yuam Tai, today the Mae Sariang district of Mae Hong Son province.</p><p>A royal official named Samdekyoi then plotted to give him the throne. While Samfangkaen was staying at Wiang Chet Lin, Samdekyoi secretly gathered forces, brought Thao Lok from Yuam Tai into Chiang Mai and set fire to the palace at Wiang Chet Lin so that the king would leave. When Samfangkaen returned to Chiang Mai he found that his son had seized the royal palace, and he abdicated in his favour.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The ministers crowned Thao Lok under the regnal name Maha Sri Suthammatilokaraja, and he allowed his father to live at Mueang Sat, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shan-state">Shan State</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>. Samdekyoi, rewarded with the title of Saen Khan, soon plotted another rebellion. The king had him arrested by his uncle Muen Lok Nakhon, ruler of Lampang, and imprisoned without harm; on his release he was demoted and sent to govern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-saen">Chiang Saen</a>.</p><p>A younger brother, Thao Soi, ruler of Mueang Fang, invited the deposed Samfangkaen to Fang and rose in revolt. Muen Lok Nakhon captured the old king, who was brought to live in Chiang Mai, while Thao Soi fled to Mueang Thoeng and was killed there. The ruler of Thoeng, Muen Soengsamkaiharn, appealed to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borommatrailokkanat">Borommatrailokkanat</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>, which began the Ayutthaya–Lan Na War. Tilokarat repelled the attack and executed Muen Soengsamkaiharn.</p><p>He went on to conquer Nan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrae">Phrae</a>, and attacked Chiang Hung and the Shan region several times without imposing his control. He faced several revolts, and had his favourite son, Bunruang, executed on suspicion of disloyalty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Alongside his wars, Tilokarat was a vigorous patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> in the Sri Lankan tradition. He built several monasteries, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-chet-yot">Wat Chet Yot</a> and Wat Pa Daeng, and enlarged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-chedi-luang">Wat Chedi Luang</a> in Chiang Mai to house the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emerald-buddha">Emerald Buddha</a>. His ashes have rested since 1491 in the main chedi of Wat Chet Yot.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tilokaraj” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom's greatest military figure was <b>Tilokarat</b> (r. 1441–1487), who seized the throne from his father and then brought Nan and Phrae under direct control around 1449.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lan-na">Lan Na · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Chiang Mai, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=18.79,98.98&amp;year=1459" aria-label="Chiang Mai on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7229 1467 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Tilokarat"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7679 1737h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7679 1737h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=18.79,98.98&amp;year=1459">What was here in 1459? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
